  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 254 AM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nA perturbation rounding the northern periphery of the mid-level \nridge and isentropic ascent is expected to spark an area of showers \nand storms across the area before sunrise this morning. Modest shear \nwill preclude any organized severe convection, but PWATs of 2-2.25\" \nsupport the potential for some precipitation-loaded downdrafts and \nhigh rainfall rates of 1-2\" per hour. Some training appears possible,\nbut any flooding should be localized.\n\nModest lift through the day today keeps a low chance for scattered \nshowers and isolated storms through the daytime hours. Temperatures \nshould closely mirror what was experienced yesterday with highs in \nthe upper 80s to low 90s. High humidity persists, creating heat \nindex values of 95-103 degrees this afternoon. A similar set-up \nappears possible tonight into Thursday morning with isentropic \nascent leading to showers and storms. Mesoscale features will \ndictate whether storms linger through the day Thursday, leading to \nuncertainty in how hot temperatures will reach. Hottest temperatures \nwill be towards central Kansas where heat index values may reach 105-\n108 degrees. A more potent shortwave ejects across the Upper Midwest \nand Great Lakes Thursday night into Friday, shunting a cold front \nthrough the forecast area. The environment will be more favorable \nfor strong to severe storms with this later round given stronger \nshear and forcing. Early indications are for a line of storms to \nform to the northwest and move into the forecast area, although the \ntiming and intensity of this line is uncertain at this range.\n\nCooler and drier air spills in Friday through the weekend as surface \nhigh pressure overspreads the area. Highs will be near or slightly-\nbelow normal in the 80s to near 90 degrees. Southerly low-level \nwinds and expansion of the ridge back over the Plains will lead to \na slight warm-up for the new work week.",
